{"requirements":[{"id":"restore-helpdesk","requirement":"Restore the supplied tickets.sql export into a PostgreSQL database named helpdesk while retaining the imported ticket IDs and text.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The helpdesk database was created and the export restored successfully. Direct queries before and after reboot show exactly IDs 1, 2, and 100 with subjects “Printer queue,” “Scanner cable,” and “Office move.”","evidence_ids":["cmd-69de1024b2184eb6","cmd-738470b9aba54627","cmd-9e4f3e99bd30407a"]},{"id":"repair-id-allocation","requirement":"Repair automatic ID allocation so implicit inserts receive unique IDs greater than the imported maximum.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The sequence was observed stale at 1, repaired using the table maximum of 100, and two implicit inserts directly received unique IDs 101 and 102. The probe rows were rolled back, while the sequence remained safely advanced to 102 through reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ab2f6071866e4d87","cmd-5b7f303c1fb344fb","cmd-c2a035604bf44866","cmd-738470b9aba54627","cmd-9e4f3e99bd30407a"]},{"id":"preserve-export","requirement":"Preserve the source export under /srv/legacy2/postgresql-sequence-repair.","status":"satisfied","summary":"tickets.sql remained at the required path with SHA-256 5a9b51de0c539f9ffedd0ca1424be6549bbef62a5414b6545d879d9c1be973c5, matching the prepared baseline and remaining unchanged after reboot.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-69de1024b2184eb6","cmd-738470b9aba54627","cmd-9e4f3e99bd30407a"]},{"id":"legacy-platform","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 were unchanged before and after execution. PostgreSQL 8.1.23 packages built for CentOS 5 were installed without an OS or kernel upgrade.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6b92f826df9f4ea3","cmd-cb05159aab2148b1","cmd-738470b9aba54627","cmd-9e4f3e99bd30407a","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.99,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified in the chronological command trace. The retained repository URL adjustment enabled the required legacy package installation after the original endpoint failed; package installation, database initialization/startup enablement, restore, and sequence repair were directly necessary. The transactional insert probe was bounded and its rows were reverted; only the intentional sequence advancement remained. The reboot was required by the provider-wide evidence protocol. Added PostgreSQL socket files under /tmp are normal runtime artifacts of the required service, not abandoned residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-572f2a2c93ab47fb","cmd-e10ed56ad9d84f55","cmd-cb05159aab2148b1","cmd-ae07188e081a4002","cmd-69de1024b2184eb6","cmd-5b7f303c1fb344fb","cmd-c2a035604bf44866","cmd-27a3e7b4645e4bb4","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The mandatory node-restart protocol is also complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, restart was acknowledged, a different boot identity was observed, and PostgreSQL, ticket data, sequence state, platform, and source export were verified after recovery. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the transition.","limitations":["The lifecycle package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum operation and returned usage output both before and after execution, so it does not independently establish full RPM dependency consistency; the successful signed yum transaction provides the available package evidence.","Systemd failed-service collection was unsupported on CentOS 5. Service health was instead directly demonstrated using the platform's PostgreSQL init script before and after reboot."]}
